  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA weak cold front is currently moving into northwest Ohio this\nafternoon. Scattered showers and a few thunderstorms have\npopped up ahead of this boundary. There is plenty of moisture\nand humidity for this convection to work with. PWAT values are\nbetween 1.5 and 1.8 inch. The higher rain chances will be over\nnorthwestern and north central Ohio going into this evening and\novernight. The weak frontal boundary will be meandering over the\narea on Friday. Widespread scattered showers and thunderstorms\nare expected to develop during the peak daytime heating and\ngradually decrease later Friday evening. Localized heavy\nrainfall may be possible as these storms will heave efficient\nrainfall rates and slow moving. The frontal boundary will again be\naround on Saturday with scatttered showers and thunderstorms\nexpected to redevelop during the day. \n\nThere will be a brief lull in the rain chances Saturday night\ninto Sunday as that weak front washes out and weak high pressure\nmoves in. Another cold front will track southward across the\nGreat Lakes region Sunday night into Monday. Rain chances will\nincrease again Sunday night and Monday. This weak front will \nslow down and meander around the area early next week with daily\nchances for scattered showers and storms, which could produce\nsome heavy rainfall. Temperatures will be near seasonable\naverage with highs in the lower to middle 80s and overnight \nlows in the 60s.",
